---
import { url } from "../data/site.mjs";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const tasks = [
  { id: "T1", title: "Map the session boundary", status: "approved", cost: "$0.31" },
  { id: "T2", title: "Port the adapter", status: "changes_requested", cost: "$0.74" },
  { id: "T3", title: "Update the call sites", status: "running", cost: "$0.19" },
  { id: "T4", title: "Add replay tests", status: "todo", cost: "$0.00" },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pi Maestro: orchestrated, reviewed work for Pi"
  description="Pi Maestro is a Pi extension. Your model plans a goal into tasks, fresh-context executors do them, and an independent reviewer approves each result."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Plan once. Execute in fresh contexts. Approve after review.</h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">
          Pi Maestro is an extension for pi. The model in your session splits a goal into tasks with success criteria. Each task runs in its own pi process on a cheaper model, and a separate read-only reviewer has to approve it before it counts.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href={url("/docs/getting-started/")}>Install Pi Maestro</a>
          <a class="button button-quiet" href={url("/docs/")}>Read the docs</a>
        </div>
        <div class="install-command code-block">
          <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="pi install npm:@ayagmar/pi-maestro" aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
          <pre><code>pi install npm:@ayagmar/pi-maestro</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note">Requires pi 1.5 or newer and Node.js 22.19 or newer. MIT licensed.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="board-example">
        <figcaption>Example board mid-run. States are the ones the dashboard shows.</figcaption>
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Four tasks on a Maestro board with their status and spend</caption>
          <thead class="board-head"><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Title</th><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Spend</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tasks.map((task) => (
              <tr>
                <th class="task-id" scope="row">{task.id}</th>
                <td>{task.title}</td>
                <td><span class={`status status-${task.status}`}>{task.status}</span></td>
                <td>{task.cost}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="board-footer"><code>⚡ maestro running · execution · ▰▰▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱ 1/4 (25%) · 1 running · $1.24</code></p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>From goal to approved work</h2>
        <p>Run <code>/maestro start &lt;goal&gt;</code>. Everything after that is recorded on a board in <code>.pi/maestro/</code>, so a restart loses nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="flow">
        <li>
          <h3>Plan</h3>
          <p>Each task gets a self-contained brief, 1 to 12 success criteria, bounded write paths, dependencies, and a tier. With <code>planGate</code> on, nothing runs until you approve the plan.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Execute</h3>
          <p>Tasks whose dependencies are approved run in parallel, three at a time by default, as separate <code>pi --mode rpc</code> processes. Each uses the model and fallbacks of its tier and sees only its brief and the reports of approved dependencies.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Review</h3>
          <p>A reviewer with a fresh context and <code>read,grep,find,ls</code> checks every criterion and ends with <code>VERDICT: APPROVE</code> or <code>VERDICT: REQUEST_CHANGES</code>. A rejection sends the findings into the next attempt.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Integrate</h3>
          <p>Artifact checks and any trusted verification command run before the work lands as one conventional commit per task.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <h2>The orchestrator stays small</h2>
        <p>The expensive model plans, picks tiers, and decides what to do when a drive stops. It never reads raw executor output. A single <code>maestro_drive</code> call spans the whole run and returns one bounded outcome, so the orchestrator spends one turn on a drive instead of polling it.</p>
        <p>The model has three tools: <code>maestro_plan</code>, <code>maestro_update</code>, and <code>maestro_drive</code>. Approval, pause, abort, and recovery are commands only you can run.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Spend and attempts are capped</h2>
        <p>By default one attempt is cut off at $5, and no new batch starts once the board's spend reaches $25. A task gets three attempts, and a drive runs at most 20 rounds. When a limit trips, the drive stops with a named reason and the safe next step.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/operations/")}>What each stop means</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <h2>Everything is inspectable</h2>
        <p>The board, a status history, raw event logs, executor and reviewer sessions, and checkpoint branches stay on disk. <code>/maestro board</code> walks eight phases from discovery to completion. <code>/maestro timeline</code>, <code>costs</code>, and <code>reconcile</code> read that evidence without calling a model.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/commands/")}>Command reference</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What it does not do</h2>
        <p>Maestro is not a sandbox. Executors edit files with your account's permissions, and verification commands are arbitrary local code that only your user config can define. Reviewers are read-only by default, and manual approval skips them. An untrusted project cannot change budgets, models, or tool lists.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/security/")}>Security and trust boundaries</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section install-band">
    <div class="shell install-layout">
      <div>
        <h2>Install and run a first goal</h2>
        <p>Check the environment with <code>/maestro doctor</code>. The default preset uses your pi model, so no configuration is needed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="code-block">
        <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="pi install npm:@ayagmar/pi-maestro" aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
        <pre><code>pi install npm:@ayagmar/pi-maestro
/maestro doctor
/maestro start add tests for the board recovery path
/maestro drive</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
